[Music]  it takes a long time to make a human we  can sometimes I think it caught up in  our own perceptions of how life works  but there are some organisms that don't  have the luxury of years and years of  childhood no for them life is brief and  they've got to get on with living and so  sometimes like with the gastro trick  they'll just go ahead and have their  first baby on the day they're born now  we've never had the chance to name a  species of microbe we name individuals  all the time this one's an eel but  naming a species seems like both a  thrilling and weighty task you take this  strange new tiny thing and you assign it  an identity that it will never know but  that other human observers will come to  rely on ostensibly forever and through  the years people who have faced this  challenge have turned to a wealth of  imagery and language to guide them  there's the multi headed serpent of  Greek and Roman mythology now also the  name for the multicellular regenerating  wonder of hydras or you could go cuter  like the tardigrade which translates  roughly to slow stepper which also has  the advantage of being accurate and then  you have gastric tricks  also known by their phylum name gastro  trikha these organisms were not named in  so flattering a fashion no gastro trikha  translates to hairy stomachs and when  you don't feel like saying gastro trick  you can also call them by their common  name hairy backs but a rose is a rose by  any other name and truth is they do have  hairy stomachs and backs all as we will  see for good reason but if their name  doesn't capture the mythological glory  that other organisms get there are  moments when the light hits just right  and bounces off their little scales that  gastric tricks resemble iterations of  one of the most pervasive mythological  figures of all time  the dragon we should scale back a bit  though  after all gastro tricks are among some  of the smallest metazoans or we could  say less esoterically animals they range  from around 60 to 500 micrometers long  though in some very exceptional cases  they might get up to several millimeters  in length there are two orders in the  gastro trick a phylum chitin odda which  are mostly found in freshwater and macro  dis EDA which are found in marine  systems though there are weirdly a few  freshwater species no matter what order  they belong to they make up part of  what's called the Maya fauna these are  species grouped by their size too big to  be microfauna too small to be macro  fauna the Maya faunal organisms are  between 60 micrometers to 2 millimeters  and they play a deeply important  ecological role a bridge between the  micro and the macro consuming the former  and being consumed by the latter they  are a part of this system that brings  nutrients out from the microscopic world  into the parts of ecosystems that we can  directly  observe castro tricks are among the most  abundant species present in the Maya  fauna in some habitats it's been  estimated that there are around 100,000  individual gastro tricks per square  meter and there are around 860 known  species counted across both orders now  that's a lot of species of gastro tricks  but it's actually surprisingly low  compared to the diversity of other Maya  faunal organisms there are for example  over 2000 rotifer species and  approximately 40,000 species of nematode  now this doesn't mean anything bad for  gastro tricks though it might be why  they have yet to be found in any extreme  kind of habitat but it is remarkable  that for microscopic metazoans 860  species is low when you put it up  against some other phyla returning to  their unfortunate but well-earned name  the stomach's of gastro tricks are  covered in hair like cilia which they  use to glide along surfaces as they dine  on bacteria algae and other small  particles unfortunately for us it is  hard to observe and film these hairy  stomachs because well they swim with  their bellies down we found that when we  record gastro tricks we have to be extra  careful their head is covered in cilia  and mechanoreceptors that seem to  respond to any slight movement in the  water so it's important not to create  any accidental vibrations a microscope  that might startle them gastric bodies  are enclosed in a cuticle and covered in  spines or scales or sometimes both and  at the end of their bodies many species  have a pair of feet which hold adhesive  tubes that they can use to attach to  grains of sand or other objects and  underneath the hairy scaly cuticle we  see the wonder  of a multicellular body a brain nervous  system simple gut intestines muscles and  more inside of this Chet Annette gastric  is an egg and when that egg hatches out  will come a new gastro trick Forgan in  place and about 2/3 the length of a full  adult the fact that it hatches  so formed is remarkable on its own but  the whole reproductive cycle of Chet  nits is a marvel gastro tricks are not  particularly long living creatures their  lifespan is on the order of days two  weeks which means they have to cram a  lot of living into a short amount of  time when they hatch  Chetta needs seem to immediately begin  developing their eggs through  parthenogenesis a kind of cloning  through which an unfertilized egg is  able to develop into an embryo that is  genetically the same as the parent in  the species and conditions that  Chettinad reproduction has been most  extensively documented in you might see  a gastro trick lay an egg within a day  after they themselves hatch and  typically they'll lay four eggs within  four days that can develop and hatch  within a day of being laid producing  genetically identical offspring and  those children are then out there  themselves laying eggs meaning a gastro  trick can become a grandmother just a  few days after it was born and here's  another weird thing sometimes they lay a  special kind of egg called an OPS a  plastic egg which has spines and is  thicker and larger compared to the other  eggs qualities that keep it protected  under harsher conditions because of this  rapid egg laying we used to think that  chetan its were entirely made up of  partha genetic females but later  observations have revealed that once  these early eggs get  the Chetta needs become hermaphroditic  developing sperm and eggs what they used  the sperm for though is a mystery we  just don't have the observations we need  to understand how Chetan ins make use of  their hermaphroditic phase this life  history though characterizes just the  Chetan its macro - Hoyt gastro tricks  seemed to be all hermaphroditic except  for one species and so their process is  of course different and yet the result  is the same the direct development of  young gastro tricks that contain the  full multicellular plan in their own  body ready to create the next generation  at birth it's hard to know sometimes  what to be most impressed by when you're  looking into the micro cosmos is it the  giant unicellular organism or the tiny  multicellular one is it giving birth on  the day you were born or the 40,000  species of nematodes of course chances  are just minutes ago you had no idea  that the earth was covered in these tiny  beasts bridging the worlds of the micro  and the macro and now we can't even  decide what the coolest thing about them  is complexity is its own complex thing  so carry on our hairy bellied friend and  thank you for coming on this journey  with us as we explore the unseen world  that surrounds us and thank you  especially to all of the people on the  screen right now who support us via  patreon these people allow us to  continue diving into these mysteries  it's a joy to do and we're so happy that  you are compelled to help us out if you  want to see more from our master of  microscopes James you can check them out  on Jam and germs and if you want to see  more from us here at journey to the  micro cosmos you could find us on  twitter at twitter.com/zeromd  youtube.com  slash micro cosmos  [Music]

